RetroBoot: the Quadra ROM's delay-loop calibration safe under TCG (VIA1 timer 2 held while the vCPU translates; m68k series).

At power-on the Quadra 800 ROM times four delay loops (TimeDBRA $0D00,
TimeSCCDB $0D02, $0B24, $0CEA) against VIA1 timer 2: it loads about 1 ms,
enables the interrupt and counts a DBRA loop; the level-1 handler discards
its frame and jumps to the code after the loop, which stores the count and
moves a table pointer on to the next loop, whose first instructions load
timer 2 again.  Under TCG those first-time instructions are translated block
by block while guest time follows the host clock, so the 1 ms deadline can
land among them: the handler then runs the store a second time with the
pointer already advanced (lab: $FFFE over the high word of the address-error
vector at $0C, TimeDBRA 0) and the ROM jumps into unrelated code.  Lab,
rb-lab: 5 of 452 cold starts never left the startup gray (the ROM's test
manager), ran wild in low RAM or aborted QEMU (insn_opsize), with and
without the other RetroBoot patches and on every disk.  This is the
PowerPC package's retroboot-via-t2-hold.patch (Mac OS 9's ROM runs the same
68k code) for the generic 6522 without the Cuda/PMU coherent-timer series:
while timer 2's interrupt is enabled, an interrupt that falls due while
the vCPU is translating waits until the vCPU has not translated for
x-t2-hold-quiet-us (100), at most x-t2-hold-max-us (4000).
mos6522-q800-via1.t2-hold (default on; off is QEMU's behaviour).  The
translation bookkeeping (system/cpu-timers.c, around tb_gen_code) is the
PowerPC patch's, switched on only by a VIA that asks for it, with one vCPU
under TCG without icount.

